  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 324 AM EST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nA digging shortwave is closing off and slowing, executing a \ntight cyclonic turn over MI today before heading east. In\nnorthern MI, DPVA forcing is decreasing from this point forward,\nand synoptic-scale moisture will be doing likewise. But it's\nplenty cold enough for lake effect showers, and those will \ncontinue into at least part of tonight. 00Z soundings had a\ninversion height temp of 0C at 875mb at APX. GRB had a somewhat\ndeeper unstable layer, inversion top 860mb at -3C.\n\nPrecip has been mostly rain to this point, but colder air is\nstill to work in this morning. 850mb temps reach -6C later this\nmorning. Given the above, and being in the unfavorable part of \nthe diurnal cycle, some mix with snow is expected this morning,\nespecially in eastern upper MI and the higher elevations in\nnorthern lower MI. (Accums will be negligible.) Mixed precip \nbecomes less likely this afternoon due to diurnal heating. \nPrecip coverage remains high into early/mid afternoon in the \nnortherly-flow lake effect areas. But coverage will slowly \ndecrease after that, faster on Lakes MI/Superior then Huron. \nHigher pops for showers linger well into tonight in ne lower MI.\n\nEarly in the weekend, surface high pressure passes west and then\nsouth of the Great Lakes. This gets temporarily displaced by a\nanother digging shortwave Sunday to our north, in northern\nOntario. This sharpens up our pressure gradient considerably in\nthe northern lakes, and sends a frontal boundary across the\narea. We are south of the better moisture and forcing, but some\nshowers remain possible in central and northern areas on Sunday,\nespecially in the afternoon. Also a breezy to windy day.\n\nThere's a hint of upper troffing over the Great Lakes early- to\nmid-week, extending into the region from northern Quebec. But\nfor now there are no well-defined waves passing thru the flow.\nFor now these periods feature an occasional slight chance for\nrain; would anticipate given the pattern there may be one or two\nsystems that do better than that, but can't pin them down with\nany detail yet.\n\nToday looks to be the coolest day of the next several, upper 30s\nthru the 40s for highs. We will become well above normal again\nafter this, especially SUnday thru Tuesday, before cooling off a\nbit again.",
